Input: {"question": "Who is Neil O'Neill's spouse?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 0, "end": 82, "text": "\"The Forgotten Union: Unveiling the Marriage of Neil O'Neill and Frances Molyneux\""}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 84, "end": 295, "text": "In the tumultuous landscape of 17th-century Ireland, where the echoes of rebellion and the whispers of loyalty hung precariously in the balance, two prominent figures from rival families forged an unlikely bond."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 296, "end": 522, "text": "Neil O'Neill, a proud Irish Catholic nobleman and seasoned soldier, and Frances Molyneux, a member of the esteemed peerage, defied convention and tradition by entering into a marriage that would have far-reaching consequences."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 523, "end": 606, "text": "The year was 1685, and the Irish landscape was ablaze with the flames of rebellion."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 607, "end": 716, "text": "Neil O'Neill, a scion of the ancient O'Neill clan, had long been a thorn in the side of the English monarchy."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 717, "end": 845, "text": "His unwavering commitment to the Catholic cause and his military prowess had earned him the respect and admiration of his peers."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 846, "end": 982, "text": "Meanwhile, Frances Molyneux, a member of the prominent Molyneux family, was no stranger to the intrigue and politics of the aristocracy."}, "D1S8": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 983, "end": 1077, "text": "Her family's influence and wealth had secured her a place among the upper echelons of society."}, "D1S9": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1078, "end": 1171, "text": "It was against this backdrop of turmoil and social hierarchy that Neil and Frances first met."}, "D1S10": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1172, "end": 1304, "text": "The exact circumstances of their encounter remain shrouded in mystery, but it is clear that their union was not without controversy."}, "D1S11": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1305, "end": 1435, "text": "The O'Neills and Molyneuxs had long been bitter rivals, their families locked in a centuries-old struggle for power and influence."}, "D1S12": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1436, "end": 1581, "text": "The marriage of Neil and Frances was, therefore, a bold statement of defiance, a rejection of the animosity that had long divided their families."}, "D1S13": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1582, "end": 1657, "text": "Evidence of their union can be found in the historical records of the time."}, "D1S14": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1658, "end": 1799, "text": "A marriage settlement, dated 1685, bears witness to the union, outlining the terms of the marriage and the dowry paid by the Molyneux family."}, "D1S15": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1800, "end": 1960, "text": "This document, preserved in the archives of the Irish National Library, provides irrefutable proof of the marriage, silencing any doubts that may have lingered."}, "D1S16": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1961, "end": 2025, "text": "Further evidence can be found in the correspondence of the time."}, "D1S17": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2026, "end": 2174, "text": "A letter, penned by Neil O'Neill himself, addresses Frances as \"my dearest wife,\" offering a glimpse into the intimate nature of their relationship."}, "D1S18": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 2175, "end": 2358, "text": "This letter, housed in the British Library, is a poignant reminder of the love and devotion that existed between the couple, despite the tumultuous circumstances that surrounded them."}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 0, "end": 12, "text": "Neil O'Neill"}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 14, "end": 143, "text": "Neil O'Neill (1658–1690) was an Irish Catholic nobleman and soldier of the House of O'Neill, a prominent noble family in Ireland."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 144, "end": 174, "text": "Early Life and Military Career"}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 176, "end": 299, "text": "Born in 1658, Neil O'Neill was the youngest son of Conn O'Neill, 1st Earl of Tyrone, and his second wife, Lady Mary Stuart."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 300, "end": 391, "text": "He was raised in the family's ancestral seat, Tullyhogue Castle, in County Tyrone, Ireland."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 392, "end": 519, "text": "Neil received his education at the University of Dublin, where he developed a strong interest in military tactics and strategy."}, "D2S7": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 520, "end": 637, "text": "In 1678, Neil joined the Irish Army, where he quickly rose through the ranks due to his bravery and military prowess."}, "D2S8": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 638, "end": 815, "text": "He saw action in several battles, including the Williamite War in Ireland, where he fought alongside his cousin, Hugh O'Neill, against the forces of King William III of England."}, "D2S9": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 816, "end": 842, "text": "Marriage and Personal Life"}, "D2S10": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 844, "end": 947, "text": "In 1685, Neil O'Neill married Robert Dunbar of Burgie, a Scottish peer and member of the Dunbar family."}, "D2S11": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 948, "end": 1090, "text": "The marriage was a strategic alliance between the O'Neill and Dunbar families, aimed at strengthening their positions in Ireland and Scotland."}, "D2S12": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1091, "end": 1227, "text": "The marriage was documented in the records of the Irish Catholic Church, as well as in the private correspondence of the O'Neill family."}, "D2S13": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1228, "end": 1396, "text": "A letter written by Neil's cousin, Hugh O'Neill, to his brother, Conn O'Neill, dated 1685, mentions the marriage and congratulates Neil on his union with Robert Dunbar."}, "D2S14": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1397, "end": 1400, "text": "(1)"}, "D2S15": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1402, "end": 1424, "text": "Studies and References"}, "D2S16": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1426, "end": 1489, "text": "* \"The O'Neill Family: A History of the Earls of Tyrone\" by Dr."}, "D2S17": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1490, "end": 1536, "text": "Eamon O'Connor (Dublin University Press, 2001)"}, "D2S18": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1537, "end": 1598, "text": "* \"The Dunbar Family: A Study of the Scottish Peerage\" by Dr."}, "D2S19": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1599, "end": 1650, "text": "Margaret MacLeod (Edinburgh University Press, 1995)"}, "D2S20": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1651, "end": 1711, "text": "* \"The Williamite War in Ireland: A Military History\" by Dr."}, "D2S21": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1712, "end": 1719, "text": "John P."}, "D2S22": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1720, "end": 1746, "text": "McCarthy (Routledge, 2010)"}, "D2S23": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1747, "end": 1820, "text": "* \"Private Correspondence of the O'Neill Family, 1670-1700\" edited by Dr."}, "D2S24": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1821, "end": 1828, "text": "Mary C."}, "D2S25": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1829, "end": 1871, "text": "Lyons